@{
    ViewData["Title"] = "Performance Optimization";
}

<div class="container-fluid px-4 py-4">

    <!-- =========================
         PAGE HEADER
    ========================== -->

    <div class="mb-4">
        <h1 class="display-5 fw-bold text-primary">
            Performance Optimization
        </h1>

        <p class="lead text-muted">
            Learn how to improve SQL Server query performance,
            reduce execution time and optimize database operations.
        </p>
    </div>


    <!-- =========================
         INTRODUCTION
    ========================== -->

    <div class="alert alert-primary shadow-sm">
        <h4 class="fw-bold">
            What is Performance Optimization?
        </h4>

        <p class="mb-0">
            Performance Optimization is the process of improving SQL Server
            queries, indexes, database design and execution plans so that
            queries execute faster and use fewer system resources.
        </p>
    </div>


    <!-- =========================
         WHY PERFORMANCE MATTERS
    ========================== -->

    <div class="card shadow-sm mb-4">
        <div class="card-body">

            <h2 class="fw-bold mb-3">
                Why is SQL Performance Important?
            </h2>

            <p>
                A poorly optimized SQL query can take a long time to execute
                and can consume excessive CPU, memory and disk resources.
            </p>

            <p>
                Proper optimization helps applications respond faster and
                allows SQL Server to handle more users and requests.
            </p>

            <ul>
                <li>Faster query execution</li>
                <li>Reduced CPU usage</li>
                <li>Reduced disk I/O</li>
                <li>Better application response time</li>
                <li>Improved scalability</li>
                <li>Efficient use of database resources</li>
            </ul>

        </div>
    </div>


    <!-- =========================
         1. USE PROPER INDEXES
    ========================== -->

    <div class="card shadow-sm mb-4">
        <div class="card-body">

            <h2 class="fw-bold">
                1. Use Proper Indexes
            </h2>

            <p>
                Indexes help SQL Server find rows quickly without scanning
                the entire table.
            </p>

            <div class="bg-light border rounded p-3 mb-3">

                <pre><code>CREATE INDEX IX_Employees_Department
ON Employees(Department);</code></pre>

            </div>

            <p>
                If a query frequently searches employees by Department,
                an index on the Department column can improve performance.
            </p>

        </div>
    </div>


    <!-- =========================
         2. AVOID SELECT *
    ========================== -->

    <div class="card shadow-sm mb-4">
        <div class="card-body">

            <h2 class="fw-bold">
                2. Avoid SELECT *
            </h2>

            <p>
                Avoid selecting unnecessary columns. Retrieve only the
                columns required by the application.
            </p>

            <h5 class="text-danger">
                Not Recommended
            </h5>

            <div class="bg-light border rounded p-3 mb-3">

                <pre><code>SELECT *
FROM Employees;</code></pre>

            </div>

            <h5 class="text-success">
                Recommended
            </h5>

            <div class="bg-light border rounded p-3">

                <pre><code>SELECT EmployeeId, EmployeeName, Department
FROM Employees;</code></pre>

            </div>

        </div>
    </div>


    <!-- =========================
         3. WHERE CLAUSE
    ========================== -->

    <div class="card shadow-sm mb-4">
        <div class="card-body">

            <h2 class="fw-bold">
                3. Use WHERE Conditions Properly
            </h2>

            <p>
                Use appropriate filtering conditions so that SQL Server
                does not process unnecessary rows.
            </p>

            <pre class="bg-light border rounded p-3"><code>SELECT EmployeeId, EmployeeName
FROM Employees
WHERE Department = 'IT';</code></pre>

            <p class="mt-3">
                Filtering data as early as possible can reduce the amount
                of data SQL Server needs to process.
            </p>

        </div>
    </div>


    <!-- =========================
         4. AVOID FUNCTIONS ON INDEXED COLUMNS
    ========================== -->

    <div class="card shadow-sm mb-4">
        <div class="card-body">

            <h2 class="fw-bold">
                4. Avoid Functions on Indexed Columns
            </h2>

            <p>
                Applying functions directly to indexed columns can prevent
                SQL Server from efficiently using an index.
            </p>

            <h5 class="text-danger">
                Example
            </h5>

            <pre class="bg-light border rounded p-3"><code>SELECT *
FROM Employees
WHERE YEAR(HireDate) = 2025;</code></pre>

            <p>
                Depending on the query and indexes, this can make it harder
                for SQL Server to perform an efficient index seek.
            </p>

            <h5 class="text-success mt-4">
                Better Approach
            </h5>

            <pre class="bg-light border rounded p-3"><code>SELECT *
FROM Employees
WHERE HireDate >= '2025-01-01'
  AND HireDate &lt; '2026-01-01';</code></pre>

        </div>
    </div>


    <!-- =========================
         5. EXECUTION PLAN
    ========================== -->

    <div class="card shadow-sm mb-4">
        <div class="card-body">

            <h2 class="fw-bold">
                5. Use Execution Plans
            </h2>

            <p>
                An execution plan shows how SQL Server executes a query.
                It helps developers identify performance problems.
            </p>

            <p>
                Execution plans can show operations such as:
            </p>

            <ul>
                <li>Index Seek</li>
                <li>Index Scan</li>
                <li>Table Scan</li>
                <li>Key Lookup</li>
                <li>Sort</li>
                <li>Hash Match</li>
                <li>Nested Loops</li>
            </ul>

            <div class="alert alert-info">
                <strong>Tip:</strong>
                In SQL Server Management Studio, enable the Actual Execution
                Plan using <strong>Ctrl + M</strong>.
            </div>

        </div>
    </div>


    <!-- =========================
         6. AVOID UNNECESSARY JOINS
    ========================== -->

    <div class="card shadow-sm mb-4">
        <div class="card-body">

            <h2 class="fw-bold">
                6. Avoid Unnecessary JOINs
            </h2>

            <p>
                Joining unnecessary tables can increase query complexity
                and execution time.
            </p>

            <pre class="bg-light border rounded p-3"><code>SELECT 
    e.EmployeeName,
    d.DepartmentName
FROM Employees e
INNER JOIN Departments d
    ON e.DepartmentId = d.DepartmentId;</code></pre>

            <p class="mt-3">
                Only join tables that are actually required by the query.
            </p>

        </div>
    </div>


    <!-- =========================
         7. OPTIMIZE JOINS
    ========================== -->

    <div class="card shadow-sm mb-4">
        <div class="card-body">

            <h2 class="fw-bold">
                7. Optimize JOIN Conditions
            </h2>

            <p>
                Make sure the columns used in JOIN conditions are properly
                indexed when appropriate.
            </p>

            <pre class="bg-light border rounded p-3"><code>SELECT
    e.EmployeeName,
    d.DepartmentName
FROM Employees e
JOIN Departments d
    ON e.DepartmentId = d.DepartmentId;</code></pre>

            <p class="mt-3">
                An appropriate index on DepartmentId can help SQL Server
                perform the join efficiently.
            </p>

        </div>
    </div>


    <!-- =========================
         8. STORED PROCEDURES
    ========================== -->

    <div class="card shadow-sm mb-4">
        <div class="card-body">

            <h2 class="fw-bold">
                8. Use Stored Procedures Appropriately
            </h2>

            <p>
                Stored procedures can encapsulate database operations and
                provide a reusable way to execute commonly used logic.
            </p>

            <pre class="bg-light border rounded p-3"><code>CREATE PROCEDURE GetEmployeesByDepartment
    @@Department VARCHAR(50)
AS
BEGIN

    SELECT EmployeeId,
           EmployeeName,
           Department
    FROM Employees
    WHERE Department = @@Department;

END;</code></pre>

        </div>
    </div>


    <!-- =========================
         9. UPDATE STATISTICS
    ========================== -->

    <div class="card shadow-sm mb-4">
        <div class="card-body">

            <h2 class="fw-bold">
                9. Maintain Statistics
            </h2>

            <p>
                SQL Server uses statistics to estimate how many rows a query
                will return. Updated statistics help the optimizer choose
                better execution plans.
            </p>

            <pre class="bg-light border rounded p-3"><code>UPDATE STATISTICS Employees;</code></pre>

        </div>
    </div>


    <!-- =========================
         10. INDEX MAINTENANCE
    ========================== -->

    <div class="card shadow-sm mb-4">
        <div class="card-body">

            <h2 class="fw-bold">
                10. Index Maintenance
            </h2>

            <p>
                Indexes can become fragmented over time. Index maintenance
                may help maintain efficient data access.
            </p>

            <pre class="bg-light border rounded p-3"><code>ALTER INDEX IX_Employees_Department
ON Employees
REORGANIZE;</code></pre>

            <p class="mt-3">
                For heavily fragmented indexes, rebuilding may be appropriate
                depending on the database environment and maintenance plan.
            </p>

            <pre class="bg-light border rounded p-3"><code>ALTER INDEX IX_Employees_Department
ON Employees
REBUILD;</code></pre>

        </div>
    </div>


    <!-- =========================
         PERFORMANCE BEST PRACTICES
    ========================== -->

    <div class="card shadow-sm mb-4">
        <div class="card-body">

            <h2 class="fw-bold mb-3">
                SQL Server Performance Best Practices
            </h2>

            <div class="table-responsive">

                <table class="table table-bordered table-striped">

                    <thead class="table-primary">
                        <tr>
                            <th>Practice</th>
                            <th>Benefit</th>
                        </tr>
                    </thead>

                    <tbody>

                        <tr>
                            <td>Use proper indexes</td>
                            <td>Faster data retrieval</td>
                        </tr>

                        <tr>
                            <td>Avoid SELECT *</td>
                            <td>Reduces unnecessary data transfer</td>
                        </tr>

                        <tr>
                            <td>Use proper WHERE conditions</td>
                            <td>Reduces rows processed</td>
                        </tr>

                        <tr>
                            <td>Check execution plans</td>
                            <td>Helps identify bottlenecks</td>
                        </tr>

                        <tr>
                            <td>Maintain indexes</td>
                            <td>Improves index efficiency</td>
                        </tr>

                        <tr>
                            <td>Update statistics</td>
                            <td>Helps query optimizer</td>
                        </tr>

                        <tr>
                            <td>Avoid unnecessary JOINs</td>
                            <td>Reduces query complexity</td>
                        </tr>

                    </tbody>

                </table>

            </div>

        </div>
    </div>


    <!-- =========================
         IMPORTANT POINTS
    ========================== -->

    <div class="alert alert-warning shadow-sm">

        <h4 class="fw-bold">
            Important Points
        </h4>

        <ul class="mb-0">

            <li>
                Always analyze slow queries before changing indexes.
            </li>

            <li>
                Too many indexes can slow down INSERT, UPDATE and DELETE
                operations.
            </li>

            <li>
                Use execution plans to understand query behavior.
            </li>

            <li>
                Keep statistics and indexes properly maintained.
            </li>

            <li>
                Test optimization changes before applying them to production.
            </li>

        </ul>

    </div>


    <!-- =========================
         SUMMARY
    ========================== -->

    <div class="card shadow-sm mb-4"
         style="background-color:#cfe2ff;">

        <div class="card-body">

            <h2 class="fw-bold">
                Summary
            </h2>

            <p>
                SQL Server Performance Optimization focuses on improving
                query execution speed and reducing unnecessary resource usage.
            </p>

            <p>
                Proper indexing, optimized queries, execution plans,
                statistics maintenance, index maintenance and efficient
                database design are important for achieving good performance.
            </p>

        </div>

    </div>


